¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQAnd when he was come into Jerusalem, all the city was moved, saying, Who is this?¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIAnd the multitude said, This is Jesus the prophet of Nazareth of Galilee.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¶And Jesus went into the temple of God, and cast out all them that sold and bought in the temple, and overthrew the tables of the moneychangers, and the seats of them that sold doves,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙvand said unto them, It is written, My house shall be called the house of prayer; but ye have made it a den of thieves.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIAnd the blind and the lame came to him in the temple; and he healed them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ·And when the chief priests and scribes saw the wonderful things that he did, and the children crying in the temple, and saying, Hosanna to the son of David; they were sore displeased,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§and said unto him, Hearest thou what these say? And Jesus saith unto them, Yea; have ye never read, Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings thou hast perfected praise?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMAnd he left them, and went out of the city into Bethany; and he lodged there.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ=Now in the morning as he returned into the city, he hungered.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÎAnd when he saw a fig tree in the way, he came to it, and found nothing thereon, but leaves only, and said unto it, Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. And presently the fig tree withered away.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ^And when the disciples saw it, they marvelled, saying, How soon is the fig tree withered away!¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚJesus answered and said unto them, Verily I say unto you, If ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree, but also if ye shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; it shall be done.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOAnd all things, whatsoever ye shall ask in prayer, believing, ye shall receive.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÍAnd when he was come into the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people came unto him as he was teaching, and said, By what authority doest thou these things? and who gave thee this authority?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙœAnd Jesus answered and said unto them, I also will ask you one thing, which if ye tell me, I in like wise will tell you by what authority I do these things.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙºThe baptism of John, whence was it? from heaven, or of men? And they reasoned with themselves, saying, If we shall say, From heaven; he will say unto us, Why did ye not then believe him?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPBut if we shall say, Of men; we fear the people; for all hold John as a prophet.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd they answered Jesus, and said, We cannot tell. And he said unto them, Neither tell I you by what authority I do these things.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙvBut what think ye? A certain man had two sons; and he came to the first, and said, Son, go work to day in my vineyard.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFHe answered and said, I will not: but afterward he repented, and went.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`And he came to the second, and said likewise. And he answered and said, I go, sir: and went not.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇWhether of them twain did the will of his father? They say unto him, The first. Jesus saith unto them, Verily I say unto you, That the publicans and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before you.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÌFor John came unto you in the way of righteousness, and ye believed him not: but the publicans and the harlots believed him: and ye, when ye had seen it, repented not afterward, that ye might believe him.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book'§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse"¦verses’„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ×Hear another parable: There was a certain householder, which planted a vineyard, and hedged it round about, and digged a winepress in it, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country:¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{and when the time of the fruit drew near, he sent his servants to the husbandmen, that they might receive the fruits of it.¦layout ªlast_verse"…¤book'§chapter«first_verse#ªlast_verse'¦verses•„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ[And the husbandmen took his servants, and beat one, and killed another, and stoned another.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSAgain, he sent other servants more than the first: and they did unto them likewise.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNBut last of all he sent unto them his son, saying, They will reverence my son.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBut when the husbandmen saw the son, they said among themselves, This is the heir; come, let us kill him, and let us seize on his inheritance.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙDAnd they caught him, and cast him out of the vineyard, and slew him.¦layout ªlast_verse'…¤book'§chapter«first_verse(ªlast_verse)¦verses’„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVWhen the lord therefore of the vineyard cometh, what will he do unto those husbandmen?¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§They say unto him, He will miserably destroy those wicked men, and will let out his vineyard unto other husbandmen, which shall render him the fruits in their seasons.¦layout ªlast_verse)…¤book'§chapter«first_verse*ªlast_verse,¦verses“„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines•‚¤textÙ;Jesus saith unto them, Did ye never read in the scriptures,¦layout ‚¤textÙ&The stone which the builders rejected,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ*The same is become the head of the corner:¦layout§quote_1‚¤text¹This is the Lord's doing,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ!And it is marvellous in our eyes?¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse*„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~Therefore say I unto you, The kingdom of God shall be taken from you, and given to a nation bringing forth the fruits thereof.¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuAnd whosoever shall fall on this stone shall be broken: but on whomsoever it shall fall, it will grind him to powder.¦layout ªlast_verse,…¤book'§chapter«first_verse-ªlast_verse.¦verses’„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd when the chief priests and Pharisees had heard his parables, they perceived that he spake of them.¦layout ªlast_verse-„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiBut when they sought to lay hands on him, they feared the multitude, because they took him for a prophet.¦layout ªlast_verse.